+// found in the LICENSE file.
+
+// Standalone spike tool -- NOT a unit test -- for AppSwap's session-
+// recording feasibility research. Answers one question: can a
+// cc::PaintOpBuffer be serialized, written to disk, read back in a
+// SEPARATE process invocation, and deserialized/replayed correctly? See
+// the "PaintOp-based session recording" design notes this tool was built
+// from for the full context.
+//
+// This tool tests TWO THINGS SEPARATELY, on purpose, after an actual build
+// + run surfaced a real architectural fact that isn't a round-trip bug:
+//
+//  1. SHAPES (rect/clip/save-restore/nested DrawRecordOp): full pixel
+//     round trip on a plain raster SkCanvas. Rendered once before
+//     serialization (--reference_png) and once after a disk round trip in
+//     a separate process (--output_png); --mode=diff expects these
+//     pixel-identical. Deliberately kept on the cheap raster path even
+//     after Phase 2 added real GPU rendering below -- there's nothing new
+//     to prove here, and switching it to Ganesh would risk introducing
+//     GPU-vs-CPU antialiasing differences that could break this exact
+//     match for no reason.
+//
+//  2. TEXT (DrawTextBlobOp -> DrawSlugOp on the wire): rendered for real,
+//     via a genuine Ganesh GPU-backed SkSurface (see
+//     RenderBitmapWithGanesh()) -- `sktext::gpu::Slug`/DrawSlugOp playback
+//     is implemented ONLY by Ganesh/Graphite GPU-backed SkDevices
+//     (third_party/skia/src/gpu/ganesh/Device.cpp,
+//     .../graphite/Device.cpp); the base SkDevice::drawSlug is a hard
+//     SK_ABORT (third_party/skia/src/core/SkDevice.cpp:486-488), confirmed
+//     by actually hitting it originally: a plain SkCanvas over a raster
+//     SkBitmap (exactly what OOP-R's GPU-process side never uses -- it
+//     always has a real Ganesh/Graphite context) cannot render a
+//     deserialized slug at all. Phase 2 closed that gap instead of working
+//     around it. Also still checked structurally first (strike data
+//     non-empty, deserialized op count/type as expected -- see
+//     RunRead()) as a cheap sanity check before spending effort rendering.
+//
+// Usage -- run as SEPARATE process invocations, in order:
+//
+//   paintop_disk_roundtrip_tool --mode=write \
+//       --paintops=ops.bin --strikedata=strike.bin --reference_png=ref.png \
+//       --text_paintops=text_ops.bin --text_strikedata=text_strike.bin
+//
+//   paintop_disk_roundtrip_tool --mode=read \
+//       --paintops=ops.bin --strikedata=strike.bin --output_png=replay.png \
+//       --text_paintops=text_ops.bin --text_strikedata=text_strike.bin \
+//       --text_output_png=text_replay.png
+//
+//   paintop_disk_roundtrip_tool --mode=diff --a=ref.png --b=replay.png
+//
+// AppSwap Phase 1 adds one more mode, for verifying real captures rather
+// than this tool's own hand-built buffers -- see RunReadCapture() below:
+//
+//   paintop_disk_roundtrip_tool --mode=read_capture \
+//       --paintops=layer_0.paintops --strikedata=layer_0.strikedata \
+//       --output_png=layer_0.png --width=<layer width> --height=<layer height>
+//
+// A real captured layer routinely mixes text with everything else in ONE
+// buffer (unlike this tool's own deliberately-separated shapes/text
+// buffers) -- Phase 2's RenderBitmapWithGanesh() handles that fine, since
+// it renders the WHOLE buffer for real rather than needing to skip
+// anything.
+//
+// A zero exit code from --mode=diff means the two PNGs are pixel-identical.
+// --mode=read prints PASS/FAIL for the text structural check, then renders
+// it regardless (see RenderBitmapWithGanesh()'s own comment for why a
+// separate GPU-backed render path is needed at all).
+//
+// AppSwap Phase 2: RenderBitmapWithGanesh() needs a real, working GL
+// implementation on this machine. Pass
+// --use-gl=angle --use-angle=swiftshader on this tool's OWN command line
+// (base::CommandLine::Init() below picks up whatever's passed to argv) for
+// headless/software rendering instead of relying on whatever GPU driver
+// happens to be installed.
+//
+// Deliberately excludes DrawImage/transfer-cache-backed ops for this first
+// spike: image resources need their own cross-process persistence story
+// (the transfer cache), which is a separate concern from the text/
+// SkStrikeServer risk this tool exists to test in isolation.
+//
+// Getting DrawTextBlob to actually survive serialization at all is not
+// guessed at here. It's copied from a real, existing, working pattern in
+// this checkout: cc/paint/paint_op_buffer_unittest.cc's
+// PaintOpSerializationTest.ConvertToDrawSlugWhenSerializationAndRasterization
+// test (play the buffer back on an analysis canvas first, then
+// writeStrikeData()/readStrikeData(), *then* serialize). Note that
+// Chromium's own generic parameterized serialization smoke test
+// explicitly skips kDrawTextBlob/kDrawSlug due to an open bug
+// (crbug.com/40223786) -- that bug is about the generic test harness not
+// accommodating text's two-phase requirement, not evidence the mechanism
+// itself is broken (the dedicated test above exercises it and expects it
+// to work), but it's exactly why this tool checks the deserialized
+// structure itself rather than just trusting the dedicated test's own
+// same-process, same-run version of this -- that part (a real disk/
+// process boundary) genuinely hasn't been verified anywhere in this
+// codebase before.
+
